From: mklab <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 43/43] TODO 17.1: the amd64 root declares two address cells, so /memory can describe the machine

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

A 64-bit firmware could SEE all of a 5 GB machine and still not DESCRIBE it.
Patches 39 and 41 fixed the seeing -- `RAM 5119 MB` -- and patch 40 published
/memory's `reg` from the counts the root declares. forth/device/tree.fs builds
one root for every arch with `#address-cells 1` and no `#size-cells` at all, so
one address cell is 32 bits and the range at 0x100000000 could not be encoded.
It was dropped, by name, on every boot:

    /memory: 1 of 3 range(s) NOT published -- the root declares
    #address-cells 1 / #size-cells 1, so an address or size above 4 GiB
    cannot be encoded here

Now, at -m 5G:

    reg    00000000 00000000   00000000 0009fc00
           00000000 00100000   00000000 bfee0000
           00000001 00000000   00000000 80000000

ONLY amd64, and that is not timidity. One cell is ACCURATE on arch/x86: a 32-bit
firmware cannot form the address either way, so two cells there would be a wider
hole to say the same nothing in. The blast radius is per-arch because the
capability is.

#size-cells IS DECLARED, and that closes a disagreement rather than opening one.
Absent, this tree had TWO answers: forth/device/property.fs's my-#scells falls
back to 1, while drivers/pci.c asks get_int_property() and got 0 -- which is why
the PCI host bridge's `reg` was a lone address cell with no size beside it.

THE UNIT WORDS HAD TO MOVE WITH THE COUNT, and this is the part that would have
broken everything quietly. tree.fs's root decodes a unit address with parse-hex
and encodes it with tohexstr -- one cell each way -- while pathres.fs compares
exactly as many leading cells of a child's `reg` as decode-unit returned values.
Left alone, every root child with a `reg` stops resolving by path.
arch/sparc64/tree.fs overrides the same two methods on the same shared root for
the same reason, which is the in-tree precedent that this works at all.

The two cells are the two halves of ONE 64-bit physical address, so the unit
string stays a single hex number and /memory@0, /ide@1..3 and /pci8086,1237@0
keep the names they had. sparc64 prints "id,lun" because there the two cells are
two independent fields; here they are not.

Measured after: dev / ls unchanged; /ide@1 reg = 00000000 00000001 | 00000000
00000000; the PCI bus still declares its own 3/2 (patch 34) and QEMU,VGA@2 still
sits at its real device number; property-abi, dict-identity, vga, client-forth,
diagnostics, amd64, amd64-pmem, amd64-linux, multiboot, persist, coreboot,
coreboot-amd64 and ppc all green.

--- a/arch/amd64/init.fs
+++ b/arch/amd64/init.fs
@@ -8,6 +8,54 @@
 
 " /" find-device
 
+\ TODO 17.1: TWO ADDRESS CELLS, on amd64 ONLY.
+\
+\ forth/device/tree.fs builds one root for every arch and gives it
+\ `#address-cells 1` and NO `#size-cells` at all. One address cell is 32 bits,
+\ so a 64-bit firmware could see all of a 5 GB machine and still not DESCRIBE
+\ it: libopenbios/init.c publishes /memory's `reg` from the counts declared
+\ here, and dropped the range at 0x100000000 -- honestly, by name, on every
+\ boot -- because nothing above 4 GiB fits in the cells the root was offering.
+\
+\ ONLY amd64. arch/x86 stays at one cell because one cell is ACCURATE there: a
+\ 32-bit firmware cannot form the address either way, so two cells would be a
+\ wider hole to say the same nothing in. The blast radius is per-arch for the
+\ same reason the capability is.
+\
+\ #size-cells IS DECLARED, not left to the defaults, and that closes a
+\ disagreement rather than opening one. Absent, this tree had TWO answers for
+\ it: forth/device/property.fs's my-#scells falls back to 1, while
+\ drivers/pci.c asks get_int_property() and got 0 -- which is why the PCI host
+\ bridge's `reg` was a lone address cell with no size beside it. Two readers,
+\ two answers, one silent property.
+\
+\ THE UNIT WORDS HAVE TO MOVE WITH THE COUNT. tree.fs's root decodes a unit
+\ address with `parse-hex` and encodes it with `tohexstr` -- one cell each way
+\ -- and forth/device/pathres.fs's (exact-match) compares exactly as many
+\ leading cells of a child's `reg` as decode-unit returned values. Leave these
+\ at one cell and every root child with a `reg` stops resolving by path.
+\ arch/sparc64/tree.fs does the same thing for the same reason (its
+\ encode-unit-upa / decode-unit-upa), which is the in-tree precedent for
+\ overriding a method the shared root already defined.
+\
+\ The two cells are the two halves of ONE 64-bit physical address, so the unit
+\ string stays a single hex number: /memory@0 and /ide@1 keep the names they
+\ had. sparc64 prints "id,lun" instead because there the two cells are two
+\ independent fields; here they are not.
+  2 " #address-cells" int-property
+  2 " #size-cells" int-property
+
+  : decode-unit ( str len -- phys.lo phys.hi )
+    parse-hex
+    dup h# ffffffff and         \ phys.lo
+    swap h# 20 rshift           \ phys.hi  ( h# 20 = 32: .fs sources compile in HEX )
+  ;
+
+  : encode-unit ( phys.lo phys.hi -- str len )
+    h# 20 lshift or
+    pocket tohexstr
+  ;
+
 new-device
   " memory" device-name
   \ 12230 encode-int " reg" property
